## Development and verification

The work model is "SDD → Feature parent → Task/Bug subissues → their own PRs." Only work that can be independently owned, tested, or delivered gets split into a subissue; a dependency means a real block/blocked-by relationship. A Milestone is instead a dated delivery/release bucket that holds leaf Issues and their PRs, not a Feature parent. GitHub Projects stays disabled. Before creating a work item, an agent searches a bounded number of open/closed Issues with concrete keywords, reads the candidates' body, comments, and linked PRs, and summarizes them to the user first; once creation is authorized, each decision is recorded in the supplement or the Milestone's `References` as retained, superseded, or rejected, with a reason. A Milestone closes only once its last open Issue closes and every acceptance checkbox is confirmed; it reopens if an Issue reopens, a new open Issue is added to it, or a criterion is unchecked.

1. Open a "Development work" Issue for simple work; titles use 12-80 ASCII characters and at least three words to describe the outcome directly, e.g. `Add dependency policy checks`. An Issue does not use the PR's Conventional Commit format.
2. Create work from the Feature, Task, Bug, or Documentation form; each form sets the native `Feature`, `Task`, `Bug`, or `Task` Type directly, and an `enhancement`, `bug`, or `documentation` Label keeps cross-Issue/PR classification. A UI creator assigns themselves; an agent/CLI creator uses `--assignee @me`. `duplicate` is a closing disposition for an existing Issue, not a creation type.
3. {% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}A Milestone Issue creates its work branch from the matching `dev/m<milestone-number>-<slug>`; without a Milestone, create it from the latest `main`. Only when an Issue records a genuine standalone soak/canary reason does it use a one-off `dev/i<issue-number>-<slug>` work and delivery directly.{% else %}Create it from `{{ branch_strategy }}`, or from an unmerged work branch whose final PR targets `{{ branch_strategy }}`.{% endif %} An ordinary work branch is created with `gh issue develop <issue> --base <base> --name type/<issue-number>-short-slug` to ensure the native GitHub Development link; open a separate Issue and branch when new scope exceeds the current Issue's acceptance criteria. If later confirmed a duplicate, link the canonical Issue and close with GitHub's native Duplicate reason; pure triage that changes no files needs no branch or PR.
4. Run `./scripts/verify`. AI follows `AGENTS.md`; review, merge eligibility, and the Alpha self-merge exception follow [08 Governance](docs/index.html#governance).
5. A PR targets {% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}the matching delivery branch{% else %} `{{ branch_strategy }}`{% endif %}, or the direct upstream branch in a stack; the whole chain of open PRs must eventually reach that integration branch. Before using `Closes #<issue-number>`, neither the PR nor its referenced Issue may have an unchecked task; risks or rollback notes go in the optional supplement. A PR has no native Issue Type/Issue Form and uses only the Markdown template; the author assigns themselves, and the Milestone plus exactly one of `bug`, `enhancement`, or `documentation` classification Label match the linked Issue.
6. Before merging, check the currently effective review requirements, required checks, Ruleset, and explicit authorization per governance; the README defines no separate self-merge exception.{% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %} Creating a Milestone also creates a `promotion` Issue, closed by the `dev/m* → main` PR once the Milestone completes. An ordinary standalone work item merges straight to `main`; a `dev/i*`'s own numbered Issue is itself the promotion Issue, and the temporary branch is deleted after merge. A standalone `fix/*` is an urgent release boundary only once it carries the `hotfix` label.{% endif %}

When multiple writable agents run in parallel, one task uses one branch and one dedicated worktree, and only mutually independent work runs in parallel; before starting, check whether you are already inside a worktree managed by the agent platform, do not force the same branch into multiple worktrees, and never delete a worktree created by another tool or one holding uncommitted changes. Native [`git worktree`](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree) is the portable baseline; [Worktrunk](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk) can optionally simplify creating, switching, and cleaning them up, but it is a local/agent-orchestration convenience, not a dependency of this template or a generated repo. A worktree only isolates the working directory; integration still relies on PR, CI, human review, and post-merge full verification.

GitHub Actions cannot see local worktrees on an agent host, so the agent that created a worktree is also responsible for reclaiming it after its PR merges: leave that worktree and update the integration ref first, then run `./scripts/cleanup-worktrees --apply --worktree <path> origin/{% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}<delivery-branch>{% else %}{{ branch_strategy }}{% endif %}` from another checkout. The script lists candidates only by default, and only accepts a branch worktree with real evidence of a merged GitHub PR, already in the integration ref, clean, and unlocked; a maintainer cleaning the whole repo runs a dry run without `--apply` first, then omits `--worktree` to apply every candidate.

`./scripts/verify` is the shared local and CI entry point. The current `.github/workflows/ci.yml` has a single `verify` job that selects docs/fast/full by the change and calls the same repo-local program; an ordinary PR does not spin up a separate runner each for fast, full, security, and an aggregate. Promotion, hotfix, release recovery, merge queue, and manual runs use full, with a single 30-minute job timeout. During development, run the narrowest focused check directly; the daily PR gate is whichever CI auto-selects, docs or fast (`./scripts/verify-fast`); only when the PR itself falls on the full boundary does the owner/integrator additionally run `./scripts/verify` once locally. See [`docs/ci-policy.md`](docs/ci-policy.md) for the full tiering and current archive boundary.

{% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}Separate promotion, release handoff, registry publisher, and deployment workflows are not enabled; the single `release.yml` is a candidate, counted Active only after its first successful live run. Version/delivery archived YAML judged not to be restored has been removed. OSV is enabled; Zizmor, remote governance, and other legacy workflows still awaiting their owner's decision stay in the central template archive and are not shipped. Reviewer assignment and the optional governance-drift schedule are covered in "Configuration and secrets" below.{% endif %}

### One-off verification when Actions quota is exhausted

A local fallback is only ever possible once GitHub Actions' zero-step billing block is mechanically confirmed and local verification passes; a runner annotation alone is not evidence. An ordinary Issue PR may leave one explanatory comment; a direct-to-main standalone merge remains human-only{% if branch_strategy == "delivery" %}, and delivery to `main` still requires both human attestation and authorization, bound to the candidate tree, with tree identity re-checked after merge -- local evidence is never used for a release{% endif %}. The complete procedure has one canonical copy; see [`docs/ci-policy.md`](docs/ci-policy.md#failure-與-fallback).

`./scripts/scan-secrets` scans the full reachable Git history whenever a commit exists, and always separately scans the current working tree; a brand-new project with no `git init` yet can still scan its working tree safely. A large repo that has explicitly accepted a narrower history range can pass e.g. `--log-opts='--since=2026-01-01'`; the full history is scanned by default.

{% if enable_codeql | default(false) %}This project has a generated CodeQL workflow analyzing the selected Python/TypeScript modules; it complements SAST checks such as cross-function data flow, but can still produce false positives and negatives that need human triage.{% else %}SAST is not enabled for this project. Language linting and OSV separately cover code quality and known dependency vulnerabilities; neither substitutes for SAST. A public repo can regenerate and enable `enable_codeql`; a private/internal repo must first confirm GitHub Code Security licensing, or the project can choose an approved alternative tool.{% endif %}

{% if enable_docker | default(false) %}This project has generated a `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml` starter, and `.github/workflows/docker-build-scan.yml`: it triggers only when a build- or scan-relevant path changes, uses read-only `contents: read` permission, builds the image only on the runner locally (never pushed), and scans it with Trivy for known vulnerabilities, without logging into or pushing to any registry or requiring any secret; the `Dockerfile` and `docker-compose.yml` are only a starting point and still need adjustment to the actual build stages, base image, and final CMD.{% else %}This project has no container template enabled. Enable `enable_docker` through a Copier update when needed, to get a starter `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`, and a read-only, non-pushing container build-scan CI; until then, no container-related file, job, or permission is generated.{% endif %}

`./scripts/verify-template.sh` and the root-only upgrade/sync tooling belong only to the central template repo and are never shipped here; this project's local and CI verification entry point is always `./scripts/verify`.

{% if not languages %}Verification currently covers spec, README, JSON, workflow, and secret-string checks; with no language module selected, no language tests or packaging run.
{% else %}Verification currently includes{% if has_python %} Ruff, ty, pytest, {{ coverage_threshold }}% coverage, wheel, and dependency lower bounds{% endif %}{% if has_python and (has_rust or has_typescript) %}, and{% endif %}{% if has_rust %} rustfmt, Clippy, tests, a release build, and a Cargo package{% endif %}{% if has_rust and has_typescript %}, and{% endif %}{% if has_typescript %} Biome, TypeScript, Vitest {{ coverage_threshold }}% coverage, and an npm tarball{% endif %}, followed by spec, secret-string, and lockfile checks.

{% if has_python or has_typescript %}
Coverage is a signal for finding untested code, not a measure of test quality, and it does not substitute for risk analysis or human review. A new project establishes a global-coverage baseline; an existing project first constrains this change with diff coverage. Adjust the {{ coverage_threshold }}% threshold only for a recorded defect, a critical module, or a real change in risk -- do not add tests with no verified behavior just to raise the percentage.
