## Configuration and secrets

Creating a repo from the template or adopting via Copier only copies files, never repository settings.{% if project_mode == "new" %} A new project's administrator must run `./scripts/apply-repository-settings.sh plan` from a trusted checkout before the first release, confirm it, run `apply`, and finally verify with `check` that immutable Releases and the other required settings are enabled; skipping this step fails the release workflow closed.{% else %}Whether release settings apply to an existing project is decided by its product-owned release process; the rest of CSARC's governance can still have an administrator run `plan`, confirm, run `apply`, and verify with `check`.{% endif %} `check` performs a read-only comparison of CODEOWNERS, the repository, immutable Releases, Actions, policy labels, and an effective Ruleset. A fixable difference fails the check; a Free private Ruleset or an organization policy restriction is explicitly marked `DEGRADED` with the specific gap recorded; being unable to read the required immutable-Releases status also fails, rather than assuming it is enabled. Use a `gh` credential with repository Administration read access to verify every governed field; never expose an administrative token to untrusted PR code. When no Git remote is configured yet, set `GH_REPO=owner/repo` explicitly. Labels update additively by default and keep custom entries; only an explicit `--prune-labels` removes labels outside policy. Copier never guesses the remote plan during local file generation.

| GitHub plan and visibility | `apply` result | `check`/PR/CI behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Free + public | Applies via REST and enables the Ruleset | Verifies `main`'s effective rules; fails on any gap or mismatch |
| Free organization + private | Applies baseline settings and keeps the desired Ruleset recorded in `policies/rulesets.json`; the public API cannot create a Ruleset | The workflow assigns one individual reviewer from the configured list and warns `DEGRADED` that it is not enforced; a team request, a red check, or missing approval cannot become a merge gate |
| Pro individual account + private | Applies and enables the Ruleset | Same as Free public |
| Team/Enterprise organization + private | Confirms the CODEOWNERS team, then applies and enables the Ruleset | Required review, CODEOWNERS, and status checks become merge gates; a policy mismatch fails closed |

`policies/rulesets.json` is the desired state shared across every plan. On every plan, the settings script verifies through the repository teams API that the team configured in the PR exists and has write access to the repo; this check is independent of whether the Ruleset itself can be enforced, and does not depend on a default branch's CODEOWNERS being up to date yet. `.github/workflows/governance-comment.yml` separately assigns one individual reviewer from `.github/REVIEWERS`, excluding the author, whenever a non-draft PR opens, reopens, or turns ready, requesting review with a short-lived `GITHUB_TOKEN` and needing no PAT or GitHub App. Copier's `reviewers` accepts one or more comma-separated candidates. Free private supports neither team review requests nor multiple simultaneous reviewers; those need GitHub Team or above. A Free organization private repo's settings page offers a Ruleset editor, but GitHub's REST and GraphQL APIs both reject creating or updating one (including `disabled`) without an upgrade or making the repo public, so `gh` cannot pre-stage it automatically. An administrator can manually create a disabled Ruleset in the web UI; `check` then reports it as `STAGED`, meaning only that it is saved, not that `main` is actually protected. After a repo turns public or the organization upgrades, rerun `plan`/`apply`/`check` so the script applies the `active` policy and verifies the effective rules.

{% if enable_governance_drift_check %}`enable_governance_drift_check` is enabled: a daily and manually triggered Action calls the same `scripts/check-governance-drift`; a fixable drift opens or updates a tracking Issue.{% else %}`enable_governance_drift_check` is not enabled: no scheduled Action or checker script is generated; enable the option through a Copier update when needed.{% endif %} Each check is a point-in-time snapshot and cannot substitute for the GitHub audit log.

{% if languages %}Local and release workflows build packages for the selected languages with the same native tools; the GitHub Release also attaches a checksum and an SPDX SBOM. Registry publishing, artifact attestation, and consumer-side gating are for the product itself to configure when it actually needs them, and are not template options.{% endif %}

A new project gets `release.yml`. When GitHub allows an Action to open a PR, Release Please creates the version PR automatically; when an upstream policy forbids it, run `python3 scripts/release_policy.py prepare-candidate` and open an ordinary PR from its printed branch/title. Either path validates a trusted author, the allowed files, version/CHANGELOG consistency, and packageability before the same workflow publishes a GitHub Release once the version PR merges; it never requires a GitHub App, a PAT, or a registry token, and no local command creates a tag or Release. An existing project keeps its own product-owned release workflow; Copier never overwrites it or publishes a second time.

The same read-only preflight also marks the optional Renovate App as `available`, `request-owner`, or `fallback`, based on personal/organization ownership and repo/organization admin status. `available` only offers the [install entry point](https://github.com/apps/renovate/installations/new); the permissions still need agreeing to on GitHub, scoped to this repo only; an organization member with repo admin still needs organization-owner approval. On unknown permissions, an API failure, or no admin access, nothing is guessed -- Dependabot and the existing CI/CD stay as-is. The CLI never silently installs an App or requests a long-lived, broad-scope PAT. Before a new repo has an origin, setting `GH_REPO=owner/repo` lets `csarc init` check the intended target; after pushing, `python scripts/release_policy.py preflight --repo owner/repo` can re-check it.

## Release and operations

Version intent, an official version, a release, delivery, and deployment are distinct stages: a PR title only expresses major/minor/patch/no-release; the exact version and CHANGELOG are written together in one reviewed PR; merging to `main` is repository delivery; only an immutable tag, GitHub Release, listed artifacts, and verifiable evidence together count as a release. Deploying to a real runtime is out of this template's scope.

{% if project_mode == "new" %}`release.yml` creates a reviewable version PR from Conventional Commits; once merged, it runs full verification and then creates a draft Release with a source archive{% if has_python %}, a wheel/sdist{% endif %}{% if has_typescript %}, an npm tarball{% endif %}{% if has_rust %}, a Cargo package{% endif %}, a checksum, and an SPDX SBOM, publishing only after that verification succeeds.{% else %}This is an existing-project adoption; its original product-owned release workflow and artifact definitions are kept, and CSARC never guesses a filename or creates a second release owner. `.csarc/config.yml` records ownership, the chosen workflow, required inputs, the settings owner, the immutable-Releases requirement, and the verification-only reason; CSARC never dispatches the product's own workflow.{% endif %}

| Capability | Current status | How it works today |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A PR's SemVer intent | Active | `fix`/`revert` is patch, `feat` is minor, `!` is major, everything else is no-release |
| Official version and CHANGELOG | {% if project_mode == "new" %}Configured{% else %}Product-owned{% endif %} | {% if project_mode == "new" %}Automatic or Guided mode creates the same kind of reviewed PR{% else %}Follows the product's existing rules{% endif %}; a published version is never rewritten |
| Tag / GitHub Release | {% if project_mode == "new" %}Configured{% else %}Product-owned{% endif %} | {% if project_mode == "new" %}Published automatically once the version PR merges and full verification passes; counted Active only after the first successful live run{% else %}The template never creates a second owner{% endif %} |
| Checksum / SBOM | {% if project_mode == "new" %}Configured{% else %}Product-owned{% endif %} | {% if project_mode == "new" %}Created, downloaded, and re-verified in the same run as the exact-tag artifact; counted Active only after the first successful live run{% else %}Decided by the product's own release workflow{% endif %} |
| Attestation | Product-owned | The product configures this separately per its registry and supply-chain needs |
| PyPI / npm / GHCR | Product-owned | The template offers no publisher option or job |
| Production deployment | Not applicable | The product defines its own environment, health checks, approval, and rollback |

In Automatic mode, a version PR created by a `GITHUB_TOKEN` workflow may wait on human approval, so the original release run re-verifies the candidate SHA. Guided mode only hands creating the version PR to a human or an agent; version decisions, candidate verification, and post-merge publishing still share the same entry point. A version PR always requires human review -- the workflow never self-approves; when GitHub refuses a tag/Release write, it is marked Blocked rather than falling back to a direct local publish.

## Template updates

First choose a team-approved version with `gh release list --repo Innoguard-Cyber-Arch/csarc-repo-template`, then get its full 40-character SHA with `gh api repos/Innoguard-Cyber-Arch/csarc-repo-template/commits/<approved-release-tag> --jq .sha`. Review that commit, then paste it into the parameters below rather than typing a placeholder directly.

```bash
git switch main
git switch -c chore/<issue-number>-update-repo-template
uvx --python 3.14 --from 'git+https://github.com/Innoguard-Cyber-Arch/csarc-repo-template.git@<reviewed-full-commit-sha>' csarc update --to <approved-release-tag> \
  --expected-sha <reviewed-full-commit-sha> --dry-run
uvx --python 3.14 --from 'git+https://github.com/Innoguard-Cyber-Arch/csarc-repo-template.git@<reviewed-full-commit-sha>' csarc update --to <approved-release-tag> \
  --expected-sha <reviewed-full-commit-sha> --yes --non-interactive
```

The CLI first previews the Copier smart diff, conflict risk, and any Milestone-description migration; only after confirmation does it run the update, `./scripts/verify`, and the repository settings plan. On a conflict, the CLI lists the affected files without modifying the repo; resolve them on the current branch, then rerun. It updates the `site/` template and rebuilds the single-file bundle without overwriting `docs/site-content.md` or `docs/site-theme.css`, and it never applies settings, pushes, or opens a PR on its own. If the project still has a pre-migration `docs/site-content.js`, that file is likewise never overwritten; the renderer keeps the old file and shows a migration notice in the output until a maintainer moves the text worth keeping into Markdown and deletes the old file. Dependency updates generally wait three days;{% if has_typescript %} if pnpm detects a publisher-trust downgrade it stops the install -- confirm the package's publisher and provenance rather than disabling the policy directly;{% endif %} OSV notifies immediately for a publicly known vulnerability.
{% if enable_template_update_notifications %}
Running the template-update workflow weekly on Monday, or manually, only creates or updates a notification Issue; it never applies or merges a change automatically. A public template source needs no secret; if `_src_path` points at a private GitHub repository, set `CSARC_TEMPLATE_READ_TOKEN` scoped to Contents read on only that source repository.
{% endif %}

## Owners and support

The code and policy owner is `{{ code_owner }}`; the governance workflow assigns one non-author reviewer from `.github/REVIEWERS`. Report a general question or a suspected security issue through the approved channel in [`SECURITY.md`](SECURITY.md); the default GitHub Issue is public, so never post secrets, credentials, personal data, or other sensitive content there.
